WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.888
The HR team at Lucia's company launched an assistant to answer employee questions.

00:00:07.188 --> 00:00:12.536
It answered confidently about vacation, expense reimbursement and remote work.

00:00:13.186 --> 00:00:16.152
The problem was where those answers came from.

00:00:16.452 --> 00:00:18.118
Not the company's policy.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.561
Just what the model knew in general.

00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:25.268
An employee asked how many remote days per week they could take.

00:00:25.568 --> 00:00:28.482
The bot said three, which is common in the market.

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:30.622
The company's policy said two.

00:00:30.922 --> 00:00:33.316
And the policy had changed in March.

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.100
The model didn't even know the old version.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.253
The first fix was pasting the whole handbook into the prompt.

00:00:40.553 --> 00:00:44.025
It worked for a while, until the handbook reached three hundred pages.

00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:48.161
Every question got slow and expensive, and the bot started mixing up the

00:00:48.161 --> 00:00:50.292
reimbursement rule with the travel rule.

00:00:50.942 --> 00:00:54.636
And this kind of error has consequences outside engineering.

00:00:54.936 --> 00:01:00.037
A Canadian tribunal ruled that an airline is responsible for what its chatbot says,

00:01:00.037 --> 00:01:04.093
after the bot described a refund rule that wasn't the real policy.

00:01:04.393 --> 00:01:09.384
In this lesson, you'll learn how to make the model answer from the right, current

00:01:09.384 --> 00:01:12.711
information, without sending everything on every call.

00:01:13.911 --> 00:01:16.772
The way out is to separate knowledge from the model.

00:01:17.072 --> 00:01:21.888
Your documents live in a search index, split into chunks.

00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:27.396
On every question, the system retrieves the few most relevant chunks, and builds the

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:31.116
model's input from them, each one tagged with an identifier.

00:01:31.766 --> 00:01:33.857
Then the prompt asks for three things.

00:01:34.507 --> 00:01:36.802
Answer only from the passages.

00:01:37.452 --> 00:01:39.342
Cite where each claim came from.

00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:43.025
And say not found, when the answer isn't there.

00:01:43.675 --> 00:01:45.047
It's an open book exam.

00:01:45.347 --> 00:01:49.152
Before answering, the student opens the book to the right page.

00:01:49.452 --> 00:01:52.038
Retrieval decides what the model reads.

00:01:52.338 --> 00:01:54.297
The model decides how to answer.

00:01:54.597 --> 00:02:00.305
And when the remote work policy changes again, someone updates the index, and the

00:02:00.305 --> 00:02:02.349
next answer is already right.

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:04.492
Nobody touches the prompt.

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:07.148
Let's follow one question.

00:02:07.448 --> 00:02:08.977
It arrives from the user.

00:02:09.627 --> 00:02:13.238
Retrieval looks up the chunks closest to the question in the index.

00:02:13.538 --> 00:02:16.095
By embeddings, by keywords, or both.

00:02:16.745 --> 00:02:18.685
Your code builds the context.

00:02:18.985 --> 00:02:22.521
Each passage gets an identifier and its source.

00:02:23.171 --> 00:02:28.360
The model answers only from those passages, and cites the identifiers it used.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:34.148
Then your code checks that every cited identifier was actually in the context it

00:02:34.148 --> 00:02:34.469
sent.

00:02:35.119 --> 00:02:37.229
And the answer goes out with sources.

00:02:37.529 --> 00:02:39.561
Or with an honest not found.

00:02:40.211 --> 00:02:44.078
The index itself is built beforehand, in a separate process.

00:02:44.378 --> 00:02:49.733
Load the documents, split them into chunks, generate embeddings, and store them.

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:53.624
And that process runs again whenever a document changes.

00:02:54.824 --> 00:02:56.449
Back to the employee's question.

00:02:56.749 --> 00:02:59.912
How many remote days per week can I take?

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:02.730
Retrieval returns the closest chunks.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.983
At the top, section two of the remote work policy: starting March, remote work is

00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:09.634
limited to two days a week.

00:03:10.284 --> 00:03:12.247
The answer comes back structured.

00:03:12.547 --> 00:03:16.435
Up to two days per week, in effect since March.

00:03:16.735 --> 00:03:19.480
Source: section two of the policy.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.128
Your code checks that this source was among the passages it sent.

00:03:24.428 --> 00:03:28.526
It was, so the answer goes out with a link to the policy.

00:03:29.176 --> 00:03:31.843
Now the math, with illustrative numbers.

00:03:32.143 --> 00:03:38.042
Three hundred pages at about five hundred tokens each is around one hundred fifty

00:03:38.042 --> 00:03:40.300
thousand tokens, on every call.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:46.256
With retrieval, five chunks of four hundred tokens is two thousand tokens of

00:03:46.256 --> 00:03:46.814
context.

00:03:47.464 --> 00:03:53.158
At a thousand questions a day, that's one hundred fifty million input tokens, versus

00:03:53.158 --> 00:03:53.971
two million.

00:03:55.171 --> 00:03:56.645
Here's one for you.

00:03:56.945 --> 00:04:00.542
The model answered citing benefits section nine.

00:04:00.842 --> 00:04:04.295
But that passage was not among the five you sent.

00:04:04.595 --> 00:04:06.294
It does exist in the index.

00:04:06.594 --> 00:04:07.970
What should the system do?

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:15.773
Treat it as an answer with no valid source, and fall back to not found.

00:04:16.073 --> 00:04:18.200
Existing in the index isn't enough.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.882
The model didn't read that passage on this call.

00:04:22.182 --> 00:04:26.340
So it either filled in from memory, or invented the reference.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.781
And asking again with twenty passages instead of five won't fix it.

00:04:31.081 --> 00:04:33.610
The problem isn't missing context.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.263
It's an answer going beyond the context.

00:04:36.563 --> 00:04:41.100
That check in code is cheap, and it's what keeps the citations honest.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.281
Retrieval isn't always the answer.

00:04:44.581 --> 00:04:49.863
If your base fits comfortably in the context, sending all of it, with prompt

00:04:49.863 --> 00:04:53.268
caching, is simpler, and avoids retrieval errors.

00:04:53.568 --> 00:04:56.804
Building search for ten pages is its own smell.

00:04:57.454 --> 00:05:03.022
If the question asks for an exact value from a system, like how much do I still owe

00:05:03.022 --> 00:05:05.773
on my loan, that's a query, not a search.

00:05:06.073 --> 00:05:11.240
Similarity returns similar passages, not a freshly computed balance.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.306
Give the model a tool that asks the bank's system.

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:19.094
Retrieval stays for written policies and rules.

00:05:19.744 --> 00:05:25.044
And if the question needs reasoning over the whole base, like how many contracts

00:05:25.044 --> 00:05:29.881
expire this year, retrieval brings back some passages, never all of them.

00:05:30.181 --> 00:05:33.418
That calls for a structured query, or batch processing.

00:05:34.068 --> 00:05:35.739
Now the common mistakes.

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:38.813
Cutting the text every thousand characters.

00:05:39.113 --> 00:05:45.028
The rule up to two days per week lands half in one chunk and half in the next.

00:05:45.328 --> 00:05:48.106
Neither answers the question alone.

00:05:48.406 --> 00:05:49.792
Split by structure.

00:05:50.092 --> 00:05:52.222
Section, article, clause.

00:05:52.872 --> 00:05:54.120
Chunks without a heading.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:59.696
A passage that says the limit is two days doesn't say which policy it belongs to.

00:05:59.996 --> 00:06:01.761
Put the section title in front.

00:06:02.411 --> 00:06:05.439
Two versions of the same policy in the index.

00:06:05.739 --> 00:06:09.239
The old one and the new one compete in retrieval.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:13.109
Tag the effective date, and filter out what's been replaced.

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:16.849
And tuning the prompt when the error is in retrieval.

00:06:17.149 --> 00:06:21.588
If the right passage never arrived, no instruction can save the answer.

00:06:21.888 --> 00:06:24.924
Look at the retrieved passages in the trace first.

00:06:26.124 --> 00:06:29.140
Evaluate retrieval and answering separately.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.897
Most problems here are retrieval problems.

00:06:32.547 --> 00:06:37.769
For retrieval, ask: for each question, did the right passage show up in the top

00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:38.099
five?

00:06:38.749 --> 00:06:43.722
For faithfulness: is every claim in the answer supported by a cited passage?

00:06:44.372 --> 00:06:49.860
And for abstention: when the answer isn't in the base, like does the company pay for

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.670
a gym, does the system say it doesn't know?

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.110
The split is what makes diagnosis possible.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:01.776
Say your eval shows the right passage arrives fifty-eight percent of the time, and

00:07:01.776 --> 00:07:04.197
faithfulness is ninety-seven percent.

00:07:04.497 --> 00:07:06.393
The team wants a bigger model.

00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:09.083
But the numbers say the model is fine.

00:07:09.383 --> 00:07:12.103
When the passage arrives, it sticks to it.

00:07:12.403 --> 00:07:16.122
In four out of ten questions, it just never receives the answer.

00:07:16.422 --> 00:07:21.719
Work on chunking, hybrid search, or rewriting the query, before you touch the model.

00:07:22.369 --> 00:07:23.187
And security.

00:07:23.487 --> 00:07:27.701
Every indexed document becomes text the model reads.

00:07:28.001 --> 00:07:32.399
If someone can put a document in the base, they can put instructions in front of the

00:07:32.399 --> 00:07:32.713
model.

00:07:33.363 --> 00:07:36.540
So control who writes to the index.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.483
A wiki anyone can edit is a way in.

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:42.401
Enforce permissions at retrieval time.

00:07:42.701 --> 00:07:46.354
An intern must never receive passages from the board's folder.

00:07:46.654 --> 00:07:49.927
Filter before you retrieve, not after you answer.

00:07:50.577 --> 00:07:54.482
And treat retrieved passages as data, not as orders.

00:07:55.682 --> 00:07:59.490
On the page, every version does the same four things.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.652
Retrieve the closest passages.

00:08:01.952 --> 00:08:05.426
Build the context, with an identifier on each one.

00:08:06.076 --> 00:08:10.915
Ask for a structured answer, with the text, the sources, and a found flag.

00:08:11.565 --> 00:08:15.556
And check, in code, that every cited source was really sent.

00:08:15.856 --> 00:08:18.062
If not, the answer is not found.

00:08:18.712 --> 00:08:23.262
Python and TypeScript call the provider directly, with a small local index for

00:08:23.262 --> 00:08:23.729
testing.

00:08:24.029 --> 00:08:26.991
LangChain lets you swap the model that answers.

00:08:27.291 --> 00:08:30.233
But in all of them, retrieval stays yours.

00:08:30.533 --> 00:08:32.925
It decides what the model is allowed to cite.

00:08:34.125 --> 00:08:35.924
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:36.224 --> 00:08:38.831
Don't ask the model to remember your company.

00:08:39.131 --> 00:08:44.140
Hand it the right page, ask it to cite what it used, and check the citation in code.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.689
And when the page isn't there, not found is a correct answer.

00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:54.610
In the next lesson, we'll turn to the first smell of the track, the one Lucia's team

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.304
almost built: prompt bloat.

00:08:56.604 --> 00:08:57.434
See you there.
